WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.189
Vernon Smith received the 2000 to

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.289
Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.269
Sciences for his pioneering work

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.129
in experimental economics.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.779
I caught up with the 96 year old

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.639
recently in Southern California

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.199
and conducted a long interview about

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.999
his life and work that will appear

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.559
as a Reason podcast.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.119
Here's part of our conversation

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.559
about Joe Biden's massive $6.8

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.559
trillion budget plan, the role

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.359
of government spending and Federal

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.059
Reserve policy in causing inflation,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.339
the bailout of Silicon Valley Bank

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.259
and why Smith believes it's

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.089
very hard to keep Democrats

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.529
from wanting to make the world

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.509
better by spending other

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.249
people's money.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.659
Vernon Smith, thanks for talking to

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.469
Reason.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.239
Nick, it's great to be here.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.240
Over the past

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.049
four or five years, starting

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.089
under Donald Trump, but the federal

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.919
government has just pumped tons

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.739
of money into circulation.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.959
Joe Biden recently proposed

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.259
a $6.8 trillion budget.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.199
You know, we're experiencing

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.159
inflation.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.319
What is the way out of that

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.520
kind of situation?

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.669
Well, I

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.320
think, the first way out

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.709
is for the central bank not to

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.599
accommodate spendthrifts

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.600
and government.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.479
Because it's

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.459
very hard to keep Democrats wanting

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.199
to make the world better by spending

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.249
other people's money.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.569
You know, they're really committed

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.309
to that. And the Republicans, to

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.229
some extent, they could

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:27.429
screw up, you're not going to be

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.949
saved necessarily by them.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.039
But the central bank, you see,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.869
they are taking, I

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.729
think, more seriously than they have

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.439
to their so-called fiduciary

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.629
responsibility to the government.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.909
They don't have to accommodate that.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.379
You see, when when

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.739
Joe Biden decided to write

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.719
$600 checks per month

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.099
for all kinds of people

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.409
who were sitting at home and not

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.239
producing any goods because

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.369
of COVID, well, that's not, you

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.289
you can't stimulus, they call it

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.519
stimulus, there's nothing to

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.379
stimulate.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.219
It's, it's, it would

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.259
be like during the Second World War

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.999
having a stimulus, while you didn't

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.489
need it, you needed the reverse.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.409
And so you know, that's going to be

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.429
inflationary.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.649
And, and, of course,

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.899
the Fed fully accommodated

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.729
that, whatever of that

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.829
debt wouldn't be held by private

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.659
citizens. They, they

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.489
bought enough from existing

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.139
private citizens that they were

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.579
willing to buy the new debt.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.089
Right.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.929
You know, the Federal Reserve

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.359
is not supposed to buy it directly,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.889
but it's a thing.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.899
And moreover, they buy,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.149
interestingly, they buy

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:38.589
that debt for the account of

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:39.789
Treasury. That's called

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:40.729
neutralization.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.869
They discovered that in October 2008

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.249
that, oh, wait a minute, that's the

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.169
way to do it. You don't buy it

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.999
and hold on in the bank because that

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.919
gives the central bank,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.179
because all of the banks have

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.069
increased reserve deposits and

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.869
they will expand credit.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.849
So you just transfer it

00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:58.850
to the Treasury,

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:02.679
it's really a Treasury on this hill.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.539
It's like, it's as though

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.029
your, your banker told you,

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.219
Nick, go and spend whatever

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.899
you want to, if you don't have

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.179
enough income about will

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.009
float bonds to cover

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.469
that and we'll hold them in

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.029
your town and our bank.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:19.119
Yeah.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.349
So that's what the federal

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.849
government—so there's no budget

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:22.719
constraint.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:23.239
Right.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.209
None on

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:25.599
the government.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.519
So recently the,

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.139
I mean, the Federal Reserve did

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.059
start raising the interest

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.369
rate and

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.919
that had the effect, you know,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.169
the standard narrative

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:39.789
of the reason why Silicon Valley

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.529
Bank and Signature Bank...

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:42.249
Yes.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.869
...you know had a run on them was

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.789
because, you know, the

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.709
Fed hiked interest rates and

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.479
suddenly the long term bets that

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.309
these banks have made made

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:52.089
no sense.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.559
And people were pulling money out.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.969
They were.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.889
So is the Fed starting to do

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:57.890
its job?

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.090
Well.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:03.879
In my view, it didn't do its job

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:04.989
before, and that's the way we got

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:05.999
the problem now?

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:06.639
Yeah.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.889
It's doing what it always does.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.989
If there's inflation, you'll see,

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.339
it starts to...

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.169
But those inflations are

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:15.999
typically coming

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.429
from excessive government spending

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:19.989
that's accommodated by the central

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.099
bank in the first place.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.139
That's where we get inflation.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.339
You see, it's not coming from

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.239
people.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.829
It's coming from government.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.689
The big stimulus for the

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.609
inflation came when people were

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.529
not home, not producing goods

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:33.699
because of COVID.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.379
And then they stimulated the

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.089
economy. Well, there's no more

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:37.719
goods.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:38.720
Yeah.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:41.859
So the prices basically have to go

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.809
up. It's just, you know,

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.519
it's just arithmetic.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.789
And, and, well, what

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.649
I predicted at the time was

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.269
that we would have a one time

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.920
increase in prices.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.099
It would continue only

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.079
if the government continued to do

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.669
the deficit finance.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.709
In other words now, and

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.269
I don't trust them not to do that,

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:06.939
you see. And the problem with

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.859
inflation, you see, everything

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.599
that the Democrats are trying to do

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.339
to help the disadvantaged and the

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.259
poor is all taken

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.269
back because those are the groups

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.369
that are so severely hit by

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:19.149
inflation.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:20.499
So you're saying when the governor

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.449
of California gives you, you

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.369
know, $100 so that you can

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.199
afford gas,

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.249
that now cost more money, you're

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.649
you're generally not making out too

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:29.769
well.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.359
That's right. Yeah.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.859
Do you think...

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.989
Silicon Valley Bank,

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.669
you know, almost immediately when

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.669
the run on it happened,

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:40.779
the government the federal

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:42.039
government said, don't worry, we're

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.409
going to cover all deposits.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.489
We're, you know, we're gonna...

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.379
Is that a good idea or is that, is

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.029
that a good rule or a bad role?

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.339
You got to think of the

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.269
consequences.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.879
You're taking all risk

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.859
out of bank deposits.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.899
Bank deposits

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.029
are the source of investment funds

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:01.809
of the banks.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.479
So they're the source of capital

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:05.589
they don't have to worry about.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:07.659
It's just really, you know,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.339
that they don't have to worry about

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.449
the default on that.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.309
The government will take care of it.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.349
And that and to me, that invites

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.479
banks to not worry very much about

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:19.629
doing extremely risky investments.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.119
And, ah,

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.189
well, there's a lot of,

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:26.469
you know, risky,

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.483
potentially high-innovation

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.559
activities that you'd

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.119
like to see capital go to.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.799
But someone's got to make a judgment

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:36.939
because there's lots of those

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.219
alternatives and

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:40.690
most of them are going to go under.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.359
So there's a room here for some

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:44.499
skill in investing.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.779
And if you take all of that

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.729
risk out of it, I think that's

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.079
extremely dangerous.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.879
Do you worry that it's, I mean,

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.099
right now it's, you know, to,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.749
you know, Silicon Valley Bank gets

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.039
bailed out.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.449
We weren't supposed to do this

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.129
anymore. Right. Because back during

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:01.469
the financial crisis.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:02.309
Yeah, we...

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.999
We figured that out and we came up

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:05.649
with a new regulation so no bank

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.299
would need that.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.889
Are you worried that it's you know,

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.499
this is just a continuation of

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.139
bailing out failure?

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.179
Well, yes, and of course

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.129
the really big mistake before was

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.259
to bail out bank equity

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.260
holders.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:23.919
The presumption was that we

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.779
were, or the

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:27.339
thought was, and I think

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.489
the government wanted people

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.229
to believe, that we were bailing out

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.059
depositors, but they did more than

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.979
that because

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:37.839
depositors,

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.579
you see, are protected by two

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.499
things: insurance,

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.719
prepaid insurance of the banks,

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:45.729
plus the equity in the bank.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.069
That's what is available to cover

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.049
as a cushion to

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.119
the cover deposit laws.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.189
And every bank needs

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.110
to take that risk into account.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.349
Otherwise it won't get shareholders.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:00.969
They won't buy the common stock.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.619
You see, unless there's a reason

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.569
that risk is, is not too

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:05.499
large.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:06.999
And if you take all that out, I

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.949
think there's no equilibrium.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.229
I don't I don't know how that world

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:12.230
would even work.

